  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of what I found and why each file matters:\n\n**Detection mechanism (Spring Boot side):**\n- `Log4J2LoggingSystem.java` \u2014 its inner `Factory` class gates activation on `ClassUtils.isPresent(\"org.apache.logging.log4j.core.impl.Log4jContextFactory\", ...)`, i.e., **`log4j-core`** presence, not `log4j-api` or `log4j-to-slf4j`.\n- `LoggingSystem.java` / `LoggingSystemFactory.java` \u2014 the `SpringFactoriesLoader`-based lookup mechanism that iterates registered factories (Logback \u2192 Log4j2 \u2192 JUL) in order.\n- `spring.factories` \u2014 the actual registration list showing `Log4J2LoggingSystem.Factory` is one of three candidates; order/priority (`@Order(Ordered.LOWEST_PRECEDENCE)`) matters for fallback behavior.\n\n**Bridge module restructuring (Log4j2 side):**\n- `log4j-to-slf4j/pom.xml` \u2014 declares the module depends on `log4j-api` + `slf4j-api`, confirming `log4j-to-slf4j` is a *separate* artifact from `log4j-api` and `log4j-core`.\n- `SLF4JProvider.java` \u2014 the `Provider` SPI implementation that lets `log4j-to-slf4j` register itself as a `LoggerContextFactory` via `log4j-api`'s provider mechanism (`ServiceLoader`/`Provider` priority 15).\n- `Provider.java` (log4j-api spi) \u2014 the base class defining the provider-discovery contract (`FactoryPriority`, `LoggerContextFactory`, `META-INF/log4j-provider.properties`) that both `log4j-core` and `log4j-to-slf4j` implement competitively.\n- `Log4jContextFactory.java` (log4j-core) \u2014 the concrete `LoggerContextFactory` that Spring Boot's classpath check specifically probes for.\n- `log4j-api/pom.xml`, `log4j-core/pom.xml` \u2014 confirm module boundaries/dependencies (log4j-core depends on log4j-api; log4j-to-slf4j does not depend on log4j-core).\n\n**Key finding:** Spring Boot's Log4j2 auto-detection checks for a `log4j-core`-specific class, not `log4j-api`. If an app has `log4j-api` + `log4j-to-slf4j` on the classpath (routing Log4j2 API calls to SLF4J/Logback) but **not** `log4j-core`, `Log4J2LoggingSystem.Factory.PRESENT` is `false` and Spring Boot correctly falls through to `LogbackLoggingSystem`. This is intentional and version-stable in this pairing (log4j 2.20.0 / spring-boot 3.1.0) \u2014 the restructuring risk is upstream apps that assume \"any Log4j2 artifact present\" implies \"Log4j2 is the active backend,\" when in fact only `log4j-core`'s `Log4jContextFactory` triggers Spring Boot's Log4j2 configuration path. Migration recommendation: never co-bundle `log4j-to-slf4j` with `log4j-core` (classpath conflict \u2014 two competing `LoggerContextFactory` providers), and rely on `Log4jContextFactory` presence, not `log4j-api` alone, when reasoning about which logging backend Spring Boot will select.",
